NOTES = {
"s1": "**What the output shows.** The training split has 5,634 customers, 1,495 of them leavers (26.5%). Ten engineered features survived the checks in notebook 02 (`price_resid` and `n_streaming` were dropped for showing no steady link to churn). The test rows have not been loaded yet. Library versions and the seed (42) are printed so the run can be repeated.",
"s2": "**What the output shows.** There are 25 fold-scores per model. Every validation part holds 1,126 or 1,127 customers and 299 leavers (26.5%), so the folds are balanced and the same for every model. A metric difference between two models is therefore not caused by one model getting an easier fold.",
"s3": "**What the output shows.** Rule 1 (flag month-to-month fiber customers in year one) ranks poorly: AUC 0.649 and 43.8% of lost revenue in the top 20% (random would give 20%). Rule 2 (segment churn rate) reaches AUC 0.822 (SD 0.012) and 52.2% capture (SD 3.6 points), which agrees with the explorer's in-sample 52%, so that rule holds up on unseen folds. The raw-column logistic regression is the floor to beat: AUC 0.844 (SD 0.010), PR-AUC 0.660, top-20% capture 56.6% (SD 2.1 points), Brier 0.1355 (always guessing the base rate gives about 0.195). Rule 1 has only two score values, so its ECE of 0.21 is not meaningful.",
"s4": "**What the output shows.** All families sit within about one SD (0.009 to 0.010) of one another. Mean AUC: GLM on engineered features 0.850, XGBoost 0.848, random forest 0.846, CatBoost 0.845, LightGBM 0.844, raw logistic 0.844. So tree models do not beat the straight-line model on this data; the joint effects they could find were already given to the GLM as features. Top-20% capture is 54 to 55% for every engineered-feature model and 56.6% for the raw logistic, a difference inside the 2-point SD, so no family is shown better on the business metric. By the rule, the three highest are tuned: GLM, XGBoost and random forest. LightGBM and CatBoost ranked fourth and fifth by under 0.002 AUC, which is noise.",
"s5a": "**What the output shows.** The search spaces: 2 parameters for the GLM, 7 for XGBoost, 4 for the random forest, each with the reason for its range. Ranges include the default values used in section 4.",
"s5_0": "**What the output shows (GLM).** In the final study 60 trials were started, 39 completed and 21 were pruned. Nested outer-fold AUC is 0.8503 (SD 0.0106 across the 5 outer folds), with top-20% capture 55.3% and Brier 0.1328. The best final-study AUC is 0.8510, only 0.0007 above the nested score, so little of the tuning gain was luck. The best parameters (C about 9.5, l1_ratio 0.05) mean almost no shrinkage: the tuned model is nearly an ordinary logistic regression. Tuning added about 0.0002 AUC over the untuned GLM of section 4 (the two use different fold schemes, so this is only a rough comparison): there was nothing left to tune.",
"s5_1": "**What the output shows (XGBoost).** In the final study 60 trials were started, 39 completed and 21 were pruned. Nested outer-fold AUC 0.8503 (SD 0.0088), top-20% capture 55.6%, Brier 0.1332. The best parameters are depth 2 (very shallow trees, close to additive effects), learning rate 0.031 with 400 rounds, row sampling 0.67, column sampling 0.46 and a strong leaf penalty (reg_lambda 8.6). The search picked the simplest end of the range, which fits the finding that this data holds little beyond simple effects. Gain over the untuned XGBoost (0.848) is about 0.002.",
"s5_2": "**What the output shows (random forest).** In the final study 60 trials were started, 43 completed and 17 were pruned. Nested outer-fold AUC 0.8476 (SD 0.0064), top-20% capture 54.5%, Brier 0.1343: lowest of the three, but within 0.003 AUC of the others. Best parameters: depth 7, minimum leaf size 14, 23% of columns per split. The untuned forest of section 4 used full-depth trees (no depth limit), leaves of 5 and the square root of the number of inputs (about 15% of columns). The tuned forest is shallower with bigger leaves, which suggests the default forest overfit a little. The gain is small: nested AUC 0.8476 against 0.8458 untuned (section 4; different fold schemes, so a rough comparison), about +0.002, well inside the 0.006 SD, so the overfitting cost the default little.",
"s5b": "**What the output shows.** Nested AUC: GLM 0.8503, XGBoost 0.8503, random forest 0.8476 (SDs 0.006 to 0.011). The final-study best scores (0.8510, 0.8510, 0.8484) are only 0.0007 to 0.0008 above the nested scores, so tuning luck is small. The history plots show the best-so-far curve flat after about 10 trials: the search found its plateau early and the remaining trials changed AUC in the third decimal. The importance plots say which parameters moved the score: l1_ratio and C for the GLM, learning rate and subsampling for XGBoost, depth for the forest. Importance is a share of a very small variation, so it says little about gains. `tuning.md` holds the full tables.",
"s6": "**What the output shows.** The GLM and XGBoost tie on nested AUC at 0.8503 (to four decimals); the random forest is 0.8476. The eligibility line is the best mean minus the best model's SD, 0.8397; all three families are above it. The GLM is the simplest, scores 1,000 customers in about 3 milliseconds (XGBoost about 5, forest about 31), and has ECE 0.024, under the 0.05 limit, so it is chosen. The GLM's predictions are explainable; its individual weights are not clean, because the bill and the service columns overlap (see section 8). In plain words: a straight-line model does as well as the tree models on this data, so there is no reason to use the heavier ones.",
"s7a": "**What the output shows (test set, 1,409 customers, 374 leavers).** The chosen GLM reaches AUC 0.847 (95% interval 0.827 to 0.868), PR-AUC 0.664 (base rate 0.265), and captures **53.8% of lost revenue in the top 20% of customers** (interval 49.8% to 59.0%), about $14,600 of $27,215 monthly bills lost in the test group; random would give 20%. Of those flagged, 67.7% really leave. Calibration is good: ECE 0.021 (interval 0.018 to 0.045), under the 0.05 target; Brier 0.135 against about 0.195 for guessing the base rate. Against the baselines on the same rows: better than Rule 1 on capture by 12.1 points (interval 7.9 to 16.9, excludes zero); better than Rule 2 on AUC by 0.031 (0.020 to 0.043, excludes zero) but on capture only +3.4 points with an interval of -0.1 to +7.6, which includes zero, so by our rule the model is **not shown to beat the segment rule on revenue capture**; against the raw-column logistic, AUC is +0.008 (0.003 to 0.014, small) and capture is -1.1 points (-3.6 to +1.3), a tie. **Reading scale for AUC differences.** AUC is the chance that a random leaver is scored above a random stayer. The test set holds 374 leavers x 1,035 stayers = 387,090 such pairs, so +0.008 AUC means about 3,100 more pairs ranked correctly (roughly one more in every 125 pairs), and +0.031 about 12,000 more. As a rule of thumb here: under 0.005 negligible, 0.005 to 0.02 small, over 0.02 meaningful. So 'a tie' on capture means the interval for the difference includes zero, and 'small' for the +0.008 over raw logistic means a real but minor ranking gain. Honest reading: the model ranks customers somewhat better than the simple rule, but on the business metric the difference is within noise at this test size.",
"s7b": "**What the output shows.** Decile 1 (the 141 highest-scored customers) churns at 76.6% (2.9 times the 26.5% base rate), decile 2 at 58.9%; deciles 9 and 10 at about 0.7%. The top two deciles hold 51.1% of leavers and 53.8% of lost revenue; the top three hold 66.0% and 70.0%. Threshold table: a cutoff of 0.25 flags 43.2% of customers with precision 49.1% and recall 79.9%; 0.50 flags 21.1% with precision 66.8%, recall 53.2% and 56.1% of lost revenue; 0.75 flags 6.2% with precision 84.1% and recall 19.8%; 0.90 flags only 4 customers (precision 100%, not reliable). The reliability curve follows the diagonal; the mid-range deciles differ by up to about 5 points (for example predicted 45%, actual 40%), which is within the noise of 141-customer groups. Which cut-off is right depends on offer cost and save rate, which are not in the data.",
"s7c": "**What the output shows.** No test segment has fewer than 100 customers (the smallest has 222), so every row can be read. AUC inside segments is 0.74 to 0.89, below the overall 0.847 in the riskiest groups: month-to-month 0.76, fiber 0.79, electronic check 0.79, first 12 months 0.77, seniors 0.78. This is expected, because within a high-risk group customers look alike and are harder to rank. Average predicted risk is within 3.1 points of the actual rate in every segment (largest gap: bank-transfer payers, predicted 3.1 points too low), so calibration holds by segment. Fairness flag: for seniors the model concentrates lost revenue less well (top-20% capture inside the group 35%, against 58% for non-seniors), and senior churn is high (44%), so check policy before using age to decide who gets an offer. Gender shows similar AUC (0.84 and 0.85).",
"s8a": "**What the output shows.** By SHAP the top five original columns are MonthlyCharges (1.51), InternetService (1.21), Contract (0.77), TechSupport (0.55) and tenure (0.49). By permutation, shuffling InternetService costs 0.230 AUC, MonthlyCharges 0.105, tenure 0.085 and Contract 0.070; every other column costs under 0.02. The two methods agree on the top group and on near-zero importance for gender, Partner and Dependents. They disagree about TechSupport (SHAP rank 4, AUC drop only 0.005), because its information is shared with InternetService, the bill and the protection counts. **Caution:** MonthlyCharges and InternetService are inflated by overlap. The bill is roughly the sum of the service prices, so the model weighs them against each other (standardised weight -1.75 on MonthlyCharges against +1.61 on fiber), and shuffling the bill creates customers who cannot exist. Do not read this as 'price drives churn'. Payment method and paperless billing show small importance here even though their raw link to churn is large, because contract, tenure and internet type already carry most of it. Of the controllable columns, Contract matters most. Structural columns other than tenure add almost nothing.",
"s8b": "**What the output shows (associations in the model, not causal effects).** For the 773 month-to-month test customers, switching them to a one-year contract in the model lowers predicted churn from 43.1% to 25.1%: -18.0 points (interval -18.6 to -17.4). Moving 474 electronic-check payers to automatic bank transfer: -6.1 points (-6.3 to -5.9). Paperless billing off: -5.8 points. For add-ons the answer depends on how the bill is treated: tech support -0.4 points with the bill unchanged and -5.7 with the bill raised by its estimated price ($5.05); online security -1.4 and -6.7 (bill +$5.04). A swing of about 5 to 13 times from one assumption shows these add-on estimates are not stable and must not be used to size anything. The narrow intervals reflect resampling of about 500 to 800 customers under one fixed model; they do not include the uncertainty of whether the model's links are causal, which is the larger uncertainty. The contract figure is the largest and is probably an upper bound: customers who accept a longer contract differ from those who do not, and the raw gap in the data was 36 points before adjusting for everything else. A randomised pilot is the way to measure what an offer really saves.",
"s9": "**What the output shows.** `scores.csv` has 7,043 rows: training customers carry out-of-fold scores (their out-of-fold AUC 0.8502 matches the cross-validation estimate of 0.850), test customers carry held-out scores (AUC 0.8474). Decile 1 (the 705 highest-risk customers) churns at 75.9% and decile 10 at 1.0%. `src/pipeline.py --train --evaluate` ran with exit code 0 and reproduced the notebook's test AUC to six decimals (0.847420), so the notebook and the production code agree. That second evaluation reproduces the same model on the same rows; it was a check, not a new choice.",
"s10": "**What the output shows.** The plain-language section of the model card is printed above; the technical appendix (full benchmark, tuning, selection, test, decile, threshold, segment, driver and what-if tables) is in the same file. All numbers are filled from the notebook's variables, so the card cannot disagree with the notebook.",
}
